<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramakien</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ramakien is the Thai national version of the Indian epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>. Older versions were lost with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767, and the standard text was composed in 1797 under the direction of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>. It adapts the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> to Thai settings and Buddhist values, is depicted in the murals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew">Temple of the Emerald Buddha</a> and provides the stories for khon masked dance-drama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramakien.jpg" alt="Ramakien" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mastertongapollo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B4%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%81%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A7_%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
